Article 42: The people's court may, in accordance with paragraph 1 of Article 39 of the Civil Procedure Law, transfer the following first-instance civil cases to a lower people's court for trial before the hearing:
(1) cases concerning the debtor in bankruptcy proceedings;
(2) cases with a large number of parties and inconvenient litigation;
(3) other types of cases designated by the Supreme People's Court.
Before the people's court transfers a case to a lower people's court for trial, it shall submit to its higher people's court for approval. After the approval of the higher people's court, the people's court shall rule to transfer the case to the lower people's court for trial.

Article 43: Judicial officers shall voluntarily recuse themselves under any of the following circumstances, and the parties have the right to apply for their recusal:
(1) they are a party to the case or a close relative of a party;
(2) they or their close relatives have an interest in the case;
(3) they have served as a witness, expert evaluator, defender, litigation agent, or interpreter in the case;
(4) they are close relatives of the litigation agent in the case;
(5) they or their close relatives hold shares or equity in a party that is a non-listed company in the case;
(6) they have other interests with the parties or litigation agents that may affect impartial adjudication.

Article 44: The parties have the right to apply for the recusal of judicial officers under any of the following circumstances:
(1) they have accepted banquets from the parties and their authorized persons, or participated in activities at the parties' expense;
(2) they have demanded or accepted property or other benefits from the parties and their authorized persons;
(3) they have met with the parties or litigation agents in violation of regulations;
(4) they have recommended or introduced litigation agents for the parties, or introduced lawyers or other persons to act as agents for the case;
(5) they have borrowed money or items from the parties and their authorized persons;
(6) they have committed other improper acts that may affect impartial adjudication.

Article 52: The other organizations provided in Article 51 of the Civil Procedure Law refer to organizations that are lawfully formed, have certain organizational structure and property, but do not have the status of a legal person, including:
(1) sole proprietorship enterprises registered according to law and obtaining business licenses;
(2) partnership enterprises registered according to law and obtaining business licenses;
(3) Chinese-foreign contractual joint ventures and wholly foreign-owned enterprises registered according to law and obtaining business licenses of China;
(4) branch organizations and representative offices of social organizations established according to law;
(5) branches of legal persons established according to law and obtaining business licenses;
(6) branches of commercial banks, policy banks, and non-bank financial institutions established according to law and obtaining business licenses;
(7) township enterprises and street enterprises registered according to law and obtaining business licenses;
(8) other organizations meeting the conditions provided in this Article.

Article 62: Under the following circumstances, the actor shall be the party:
(1) a legal person or other organization that should have registered but has not registered, and the actor engages in civil activities in the name of the legal person or other organization;
(2) the actor engages in civil activities in the name of the principal without agency authority, beyond the agency authority, or after the agency authority has terminated, except where the counterparty has reason to believe that the actor has agency authority;
(3) after a legal person or other organization has been terminated according to law, the actor still engages in civil activities in its name.

Article 87. According to Item 3, Paragraph 2, Article 61 of the Civil Procedure Law, where a relevant social organization recommends a citizen to serve as a litigation agent, the following conditions shall be met:
(1) The social organization is a non-profit legal person organization established upon registration according to law or exempted from registration according to law;
(2) The principal is a member of the social organization, or the domicile of one party is located within the activity area of the social organization;
(3) The agency matter is within the business scope specified in the articles of association of the social organization;
(4) The recommended citizen is a person in charge of the social organization or a staff member who has a lawful labor or personnel relationship with the social organization.
A patent agent, upon recommendation by the All-China Patent Agents Association, may serve as a litigation agent in patent dispute cases.

Article 88. In addition to submitting the power of attorney in accordance with Article 62 of the Civil Procedure Law, a litigation agent shall also submit relevant materials to the People's Court in accordance with the following provisions:
(1) A lawyer shall submit his or her lawyer's practice certificate and certification materials of the law firm;
(2) A grassroots legal service worker shall submit his or her legal service worker's practice certificate, a letter of introduction issued by the grassroots legal service office, and certification materials proving that one party is located within its jurisdiction;
(3) A close relative of a party shall submit identification documents and certification materials proving the close relative relationship with the principal;
(4) A staff member of a party shall submit identification documents and certification materials proving a lawful labor or personnel relationship with the party;
(5) A citizen recommended by the community or unit where the party is located shall submit identification documents, recommendation materials, and certification materials proving that the party belongs to such community or unit;
(6) A citizen recommended by a relevant social organization shall submit identification documents and certification materials meeting the conditions provided in Article 87 of this Interpretation.

Article 91. The People's Court shall determine the bearing of the burden of proof in accordance with the following principles, except as otherwise provided by law:
(1) A party who asserts the existence of a legal relationship shall bear the burden of proof with respect to the basic facts that give rise to such legal relationship;
(2) A party who asserts a change or extinguishment of a legal relationship, or an obstruction of rights, shall bear the burden of proof with respect to the basic facts of such change, extinguishment, or obstruction of rights.

Article 93. The following facts need not be proved by the parties:
(1) Natural laws, theorems, and laws;
(2) Facts known to the general public;
(3) Facts presumed in accordance with legal provisions;
(4) Facts presumed from known facts and rules of daily life experience;
(5) Facts confirmed by legally effective judgments or rulings of the People's Court;
(6) Facts confirmed by effective arbitral awards of arbitration institutions;
(7) Facts proved by effective notarized documents.
Unless the parties have contrary evidence sufficient to rebut the facts provided in Items 2 through 4 of the preceding paragraph, or sufficient to overturn the facts provided in Items 5 through 7.

Article 94. The evidence that a party and his or her litigation agent cannot collect on their own due to objective reasons as provided in Article 67, Paragraph 2 of the Civil Procedure Law includes:
(1) Evidence kept by relevant state authorities, to which the party and his or her litigation agent have no right to access and obtain;
(2) Evidence involving state secrets, trade secrets, or personal privacy;
(3) Other evidence that the party and his or her litigation agent cannot collect on their own due to objective reasons.
For evidence that the party and his or her litigation agent cannot collect on their own due to objective reasons, a written application may be submitted to the People's Court for investigation and collection before the expiration of the time limit for evidence production.

Article 96. The evidence that the People's Court considers necessary for the trial of a case as provided in Article 67, Paragraph 2 of the Civil Procedure Law includes:
(1) Evidence that may concern damage to national interests or social public interests;
(2) Evidence involving personal status relationships;
(3) Evidence involving an action provided in Article 58 of the Civil Procedure Law;
(4) Where the parties may maliciously collude to damage the lawful rights and interests of others;
(5) Evidence involving procedural matters such as adding parties ex officio, suspending litigation, terminating litigation, or recusal.
Except as provided in the preceding paragraph, the People's Court shall investigate and collect evidence upon the application of the parties.

Article 111. The 'genuine difficulty' in submitting the original of documentary evidence as provided in Article 73 of the Civil Procedure Law includes the following circumstances:
(1) The original document is lost, destroyed, or damaged;
(2) The original is under the control of the opposing party, and the opposing party refuses to submit it after being lawfully notified to do so;
(3) The original is under the control of another person, and such person has the right not to submit it;
(4) The original is inconvenient to submit due to its length or volume;
(5) The party bearing the burden of proof is unable to obtain the original documentary evidence through applying to the People's Court for investigation and collection or through other means.
Under the circumstances provided in the preceding paragraph, the People's Court shall, in light of other evidence and the specific circumstances of the case, examine and determine whether copies of the documentary evidence may be used as the basis for ascertaining the facts of the case.

Article 166 After ruling to take preservation measures, under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall make a ruling to dissolve the preservation:
(1) the preservation is erroneous;
(2) the applicant withdraws the preservation application;
(3) the applicant's prosecution or litigation claims are rejected by an effective judgment or ruling;
(4) there are other circumstances under which the People's Court considers the preservation shall be dissolved.
To dissolve preservation measures implemented by way of registration, a notice to assist in enforcement shall be issued to the registration authority.

Article 170 The urgent circumstances provided for in item (3) of Article 109 of the Civil Procedure Law include:
(1) where it is necessary to immediately stop the infringement or remove the obstruction;
(2) where it is necessary to immediately stop a certain act;
(3) where claiming insurance compensation urgently needed for the resumption of production or business operations;
(4) where it is necessary to immediately return social insurance benefits or social assistance funds;
(5) where failure to immediately return the funds would seriously affect the right holder's livelihood, production, or business operations.

Article 176. Where any litigation participant or other person commits any of the following acts, the People's Court may handle the matter in accordance with the provisions of Article 113 of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) making audio recordings, video recordings, or taking photographs without permission;
(2) broadcasting trial activities on site by means of mobile communications or otherwise without permission;
(3) other acts disturbing court order and obstructing trial proceedings.
In any of the circumstances set forth in the preceding paragraph, the People's Court may temporarily seize the equipment used by the litigation participant or other person for audio recording, video recording, photographing, or broadcasting trial activities, and order the deletion of the relevant content; if the person refuses to delete, the People's Court may take necessary measures to force deletion.

Article 187. The acts of using violence, threats, or other methods to obstruct judicial personnel in performing official duties as provided in item 5 of paragraph 1 of Article 114 of the Civil Procedure Law include:
(1) creating a disturbance or lingering in the People's Court and refusing to heed the dissuasion of judicial personnel;
(2) intentionally destroying or seizing the legal instruments or sealing marks of the People's Court;
(3) creating a disturbance at or storming the scene of official enforcement, or besieging or detaining persons executing or assisting in executing official duties;
(4) destroying, seizing, or detaining case materials, official enforcement vehicles, other enforcement instruments, uniforms of enforcement personnel, or enforcement credentials;
(5) using violence, threats, or other methods to obstruct judicial personnel from inquiring, sealing, seizing, freezing, transferring, auctioning, or selling property;
(6) other acts that use violence, threats, or other methods to obstruct judicial personnel in performing official duties.

Article 188. The acts of refusing to perform a judgment or ruling of the People's Court that has taken legal effect as provided in item 6 of paragraph 1 of Article 114 of the Civil Procedure Law include:
(1) after the legal instrument takes legal effect, concealing, transferring, selling off, or destroying property, or gratuitously transferring property, trading property at an obviously unreasonable price, abandoning due claims, gratuitously providing security for others, etc., thereby making it impossible for the People's Court to enforce;
(2) concealing, transferring, destroying, or disposing of, without permission of the People's Court, property that has been provided as security to the People's Court;
(3) consuming in violation of the People's Court's order restricting high consumption;
(4) having the ability to perform but refusing to perform the obligations determined by the effective legal instrument in accordance with the enforcement notice of the People's Court;
(5) where an individual obligated to assist in enforcement, after receiving the notice of assistance in enforcement from the People's Court, refuses to assist in enforcement.

Article 189. Where any litigation participant or other person commits any of the following acts, the People's Court may handle the matter in accordance with the provisions of Article 114 of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) impersonating another person to bring an action or participate in litigation;
(2) a witness making false testimony after signing an undertaking, thereby obstructing the People's Court in hearing the case;
(3) forging, concealing, destroying, or refusing to surrender important evidence concerning the property execution ability of the person subject to execution, thereby obstructing the People's Court from ascertaining the property status of the person subject to execution;
(4) unfreezing, without authorization, property that has been frozen by the People's Court;
(5) after receiving the notice of assistance in enforcement from the People's Court, tipping off the party and assisting the party in transferring or concealing property.

Article 192. Where the relevant unit, after receiving a notice of assistance in enforcement from the People's Court, commits any of the following acts, the People's Court may handle the matter in accordance with the provisions of Article 117 of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) permitting the person subject to execution to engage in high consumption;
(2) permitting the person subject to execution to leave the country;
(3) refusing to cease handling procedures such as transfer of property right certificates, change of ownership registration, and planning approval;
(4) delaying the handling on the grounds that internal instructions, internal approvals, or internal regulations are required.

Article 216. Before the first hearing in the People's Court, if the defendant raises an objection to the acceptance of a civil case on the ground that there is a written arbitration agreement, the People's Court shall examine the matter.
If, upon examination, any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Court shall, by ruling, dismiss the action:
(1) the arbitration institution or the People's Court has already confirmed that the arbitration agreement is valid;
(2) the parties have not raised an objection to the validity of the arbitration agreement before the first hearing of the arbitral tribunal;
(3) the arbitration agreement conforms to the provisions of Article 16 of the Arbitration Law and does not fall under the circumstances specified in Article 17 of the Arbitration Law.

Article 225: Depending on the specific circumstances of the case, the pre-trial conference may include the following contents:
(1) clarifying the plaintiff's claims and the defendant's defense opinions;
(2) examining and handling the parties' applications to add or modify claims and the counterclaim filed, as well as claims related to the case raised by a third party;
(3) deciding, upon the parties' application, to investigate and collect evidence, entrust expert evaluation, require the parties to provide evidence, conduct inspection, and preserve evidence;
(4) organizing evidence exchange;
(5) summarizing the disputed issues;
(6) conducting mediation.

Article 247. Where a party files another action on a matter for which an action has already been instituted during the proceedings or after the judgment or ruling takes legal effect, and the following conditions are concurrently met, it constitutes a duplicate action:
(1) the parties in the subsequent action are the same as those in the previous action;
(2) the subject matter of the subsequent action is the same as that of the previous action;
(3) the claims in the subsequent action are the same as those in the previous action, or the claims in the subsequent action substantially negate the adjudication result of the previous action.
Where a party files a duplicate action, the People's Court shall rule not to accept it; if it has already been accepted, it shall rule to dismiss the action, except as otherwise provided by law or judicial interpretation.

Article 252. For a case in which a retrial ruling revokes the original judgment or ruling and remands the case for retrial, the People's Court shall permit a party to modify or add claims or file a counterclaim in any of the following circumstances:
(1) the original trial rendered a default judgment without lawful summons, affecting the party's exercise of litigation rights;
(2) new parties to the action are added;
(3) the subject matter of the action is destroyed or changed, making the original claims impossible to realize;
(4) the claims that the party applies to modify or add or the counterclaim filed cannot be resolved through a separate action.

Article 255. For an application to inspect a judgment or ruling, the People's Court shall handle it separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) if the judgment or ruling has already been disclosed to the public through the information network, the applicant shall be guided to inspect it on its own;
(2) if the judgment or ruling has not been disclosed to the public through the information network and the application meets the requirements, convenient inspection services shall be provided in a timely manner;
(3) if the judgment or ruling has not yet taken legal effect or has lost legal effect, inspection shall not be provided and the applicant shall be so informed;
(4) if the legally effective judgment or ruling was not rendered by this court, the applicant shall be informed to apply to the People's Court that rendered the effective adjudication for inspection;
(5) if the content requested for inspection involves state secrets, trade secrets, or personal privacy, the application shall not be permitted and the applicant shall be so informed.

Article 257. The following cases shall not be tried under the summary procedure:
(1) where the whereabouts of the defendant are unknown at the time of filing the action;
(2) cases remanded for retrial;
(3) cases where one party consists of a large number of persons;
(4) cases under the trial supervision procedure;
(5) cases involving state interests or social public interests;
(6) cases where a third party files an action requesting modification or revocation of an effective judgment, ruling, or mediation statement;
(7) other cases not suitable for the summary procedure.

Article 263. For a case tried under the summary procedure, the case file shall contain the following materials:
(1) the complaint or the record of oral complaint;
(2) the statement of defense or the record of oral defense;
(3) the identification materials of the parties;
(4) the power of attorney for a person entrusted to act as agent in the litigation, or the record of oral entrustment;
(5) evidence;
(6) records of questioning the parties;
(7) records of the hearing (including mediation);
(8) judgments, rulings, mediation statements, or mediation agreements;
(9) records of service and pronouncement;
(10) the status of enforcement;
(11) receipts of litigation costs;
(12) where the case is tried in accordance with Article 165 of the Civil Procedure Law, a written notice concerning the application of the relevant procedure.

Article 270. For a case tried under the summary procedure, where any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Court may appropriately simplify the part on factual findings or adjudication reasons when producing a judgment, ruling, or mediation statement:
(1) the parties reach a mediation agreement and a civil mediation statement needs to be produced;
(2) one party expressly admits all or part of the claims of the other party;
(3) in a case involving trade secrets or personal privacy, one party requests simplification of the relevant contents in the adjudication document and the People's Court considers the reasons justified;
(4) both parties agree to simplification.

Article 282. Where organs and relevant organizations provided by laws such as the Environmental Protection Law and the Law on the Protection of Consumer Rights and Interests, in accordance with Article 58 of the Civil Procedure Law, file a public interest action against acts that damage social public interests, such as polluting the environment or infringing upon the lawful rights and interests of numerous consumers, the People's Court shall accept the action if the following conditions are met:
(1) there is a definite defendant;
(2) there are specific claims;
(3) there is preliminary evidence that social public interests have been damaged;
(4) the case falls within the scope of civil actions accepted by the People's Court and under the jurisdiction of the People's Court accepting the action.

Article 290. Where a third party initiates an action to set aside a legally effective judgment, ruling, or mediation statement, the third party shall file the action with the People's Court that rendered the effective judgment, ruling, or mediation statement within six months from the date on which the third party knew or should have known that its civil rights and interests were harmed, and shall provide evidentiary materials proving the existence of the following circumstances:
(1) failure to participate in the proceedings for reasons not attributable to oneself;
(2) errors in all or part of the contents of the legally effective judgment, ruling, or mediation statement;
(3) errors in the contents of the legally effective judgment, ruling, or mediation statement harming its civil rights and interests.

Article 293. The failure to participate in the proceedings for reasons not attributable to oneself as provided for in Article 59, paragraph 3 of the Civil Procedure Law means a circumstance in which a person was not listed as a party to the effective judgment, ruling, or mediation statement and was not at fault or was not obviously at fault. It includes:
(1) failing to participate because of unawareness of the litigation;
(2) applying to participate but not being permitted;
(3) being aware of the litigation but unable to participate due to objective reasons;
(4) failing to participate for other reasons not attributable to oneself.

Article 295. The People's Court shall not accept an action to set aside filed under any of the following circumstances:
(1) cases handled under non-contentious procedures such as special procedures, supervision procedures, public summons procedures, or bankruptcy procedures;
(2) contents involving personal status relations in judgments, rulings, or mediation statements such as those on nullity of marriage, revocation of marriage, or dissolution of marital relationship;
(3) effective adjudications in representative action cases brought by rights holders who did not register to participate as provided for in Article 57 of the Civil Procedure Law;
(4) effective adjudications in public interest litigation cases brought by victims of acts that harm social public interests as provided for in Article 58 of the Civil Procedure Law.

Article 298. With respect to a third party's request to set aside in whole or in part the contents of a legally effective judgment, ruling, or mediation statement, the People's Court shall, after trial, handle the matter separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the request is established and the claims for confirmation of its civil rights are established in whole or in part, the erroneous parts of the original judgment, ruling, or mediation statement shall be modified;
(2) If the request is established but the claims for confirmation of all or part of its civil rights are not established, or no claim for confirmation of its civil rights is raised, the erroneous parts of the original judgment, ruling, or mediation statement shall be set aside;
(3) If the request is not established, the litigation claim shall be rejected.
A party may appeal against an adjudication under the preceding paragraph.
The parts of the original judgment, ruling, or mediation statement that are not modified or set aside shall continue to be effective.

Article 300. Where a third party's litigation claims are consolidated into the retrial procedure for trial, they shall be handled separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the case is tried under the first-instance procedure, the People's Court shall try the third party's litigation claims together, and the judgment rendered may be appealed;
(2) If the case is tried under the second-instance procedure, the People's Court may mediate; if mediation fails to reach an agreement, the People's Court shall rule to set aside the original judgment, ruling, or mediation statement and remand the case to the first-instance court for retrial, during which the third party shall be listed.

Article 303. To initiate an enforcement objection action, a person other than a party shall, in addition to meeting the requirements of Article 122 of the Civil Procedure Law, satisfy the following conditions:
(1) the application for enforcement objection by the person other than a party has been rejected by a ruling of the People's Court;
(2) there is a clear litigation claim to exclude enforcement against the subject matter of enforcement, and the litigation claim is unrelated to the original judgment or ruling;
(3) the action is initiated within fifteen days from the date of service of the ruling on the enforcement objection.
The People's Court shall decide whether to docket the case within fifteen days from the date of receipt of the complaint.

Article 304. To initiate an enforcement objection action, the enforcement applicant shall, in addition to meeting the requirements of Article 122 of the Civil Procedure Law, satisfy the following conditions:
(1) the People's Court has ruled to suspend enforcement upon the application for enforcement objection by the person other than a party;
(2) there is a clear litigation claim to continue enforcement against the subject matter of enforcement, and the litigation claim is unrelated to the original judgment or ruling;
(3) the action is initiated within fifteen days from the date of service of the ruling on the enforcement objection.
The People's Court shall decide whether to docket the case within fifteen days from the date of receipt of the complaint.

Article 311. With respect to an enforcement objection action initiated by the enforcement applicant, the People's Court shall, after trial, handle the matter separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the person other than a party does not have civil rights and interests in the subject matter of enforcement sufficient to exclude compulsory enforcement, it shall be adjudicated that enforcement of the subject matter of enforcement is permitted;
(2) If the person other than a party has civil rights and interests in the subject matter of enforcement sufficient to exclude compulsory enforcement, the litigation claim shall be rejected.

Article 317. Where one or some of the necessary co-litigants file an appeal, the matter shall be handled separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the appeal only objects to the allocation of rights and obligations with the other party and does not involve the interests of other co-litigants, the other party shall be the respondent, and the non-appealing parties on the same side shall be listed according to their litigation status in the original trial;
(2) If the appeal only objects to the allocation of rights and obligations among the co-litigants and does not involve the interests of the other party, the non-appealing parties on the same side shall be the respondents, and the other party shall be listed according to its litigation status in the original trial;
(3) If the appeal objects to the assumption of rights and obligations between the parties and among the co-litigants, all other parties who did not file an appeal shall be respondents.

Article 323. The following circumstances may be found to constitute serious violation of statutory procedures as provided for in item (4) of paragraph 1 of Article 177 of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) the composition of the adjudication organization is unlawful;
(2) a judicial officer who should have withdrawn failed to withdraw;
(3) a person without capacity for litigation did not have a statutory agent to act on his or her behalf in the litigation;
(4) the parties' right to debate was unlawfully deprived.

Article 331. The People's Court of second instance may, in accordance with Article 176 of the Civil Procedure Law, try the following appeal cases without a hearing:
(1) appeals against rulings on non-acceptance, jurisdictional objection, and dismissal of action;
(2) cases where the appellant's claims on appeal are clearly untenable;
(3) cases where the facts found in the original judgment or ruling are clear but the law was incorrectly applied;
(4) cases where the original judgment seriously violates statutory procedures and needs to be remanded for retrial.

Article 345. The public announcement for seeking the whereabouts of a missing person shall record the following contents:
(1) The respondent shall, within the specified period, report his or her specific address and contact information to the accepting court. Otherwise, the respondent shall be declared missing or declared dead;
(2) Any person who knows the current living status of the respondent shall, within the public announcement period, report what he or she knows to the accepting court.

Article 355. Where a party applies for judicial confirmation of a mediation agreement, in any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall rule not to accept the application:
(1) The matter is not within the scope of cases accepted by the People's Court;
(2) The matter is not within the jurisdiction of the People's Court that received the application;
(3) The application is for confirmation of the invalidity, validity, or dissolution of status relationships such as marital relationship, parent-child relationship, or adoption relationship;
(4) The matter involves adjudication under other special procedures, proceedings for public summons, or bankruptcy proceedings;
(5) The content of the mediation agreement involves confirmation of rights in rem or intellectual property rights.
After accepting the application, if the People's Court discovers any of the above circumstances for non-acceptance, it shall rule to reject the party's application.

Article 358. Upon examination, if the mediation agreement has any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall rule to reject the application:
(1) It violates mandatory provisions of the law;
(2) It harms national interests, public interests, or the lawful rights and interests of others;
(3) It violates public order and good morals;
(4) It violates the principle of voluntariness;
(5) Its content is unclear;
(6) Other circumstances where judicial confirmation cannot be made.

Article 365. To apply for realization of a security interest, the following materials shall be submitted:
(1) A written application. The application shall state the basic information such as the names or titles and contact information of the applicant and the respondent, the specific claims, and the facts and reasons;
(2) Materials proving the existence of the security interest, including the main contract, the security contract, mortgage registration certificate or certificate of other rights, right certificates for pledge of rights, or pledge registration certificate, etc.;
(3) Materials proving that the conditions for realization of the security interest have been satisfied;
(4) A description of the current status of the security property;
(5) Other materials that the People's Court considers necessary to be submitted.

Article 370. After examination, the People's Court shall handle the case separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the parties have no substantive dispute over the realization of the security interest and the conditions for realization are satisfied, it shall rule to permit the auction or sale of the security property;
(2) If the parties have partial substantive disputes over the realization of the security interest, it may rule to permit the auction or sale of the security property with respect to the undisputed part;
(3) If the parties have substantive disputes over the realization of the security interest, it shall rule to reject the application and inform the applicant to file an action with the People's Court.

Article 375. A party applying for retrial shall submit the following materials:
(1) A written application for retrial, together with copies in the number equal to the number of respondents and other parties in the original trial;
(2) If the applicant for retrial is a natural person, he or she shall submit an identity certificate; if the applicant for retrial is a legal person or other organization, it shall submit a business license, organization code certificate, and certification of the identity of the legal representative or the principal person in charge. If another person is entrusted to make the application, a power of attorney and the agent's identity certificate shall be submitted;
(3) The judgment, ruling, or mediation statement of the original trial;
(4) The main evidence reflecting the basic facts of the case and other materials.
The materials provided in items (2), (3), and (4) of the preceding paragraph may be copies that have been verified against the originals.

Article 376. A written application for retrial shall state the following matters:
(1) The basic information of the applicant for retrial, the respondent, and other parties in the original trial;
(2) The name of the People's Court of the original trial and the case number of the original adjudication document;
(3) The specific claims for retrial;
(4) The statutory circumstances for applying for retrial and the specific facts and reasons.
The written application for retrial shall identify the People's Court to which the application for retrial is made, and shall be signed, fingerprinted, or sealed by the applicant for retrial.

Article 381. Where a party applies for retrial, in any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall not accept the application:
(1) An application is filed again after a previous application for retrial has been rejected;
(2) An application is filed against a retrial judgment or ruling;
(3) An application is filed again after the People's Procuratorate has made a decision not to issue a procuratorial recommendation for retrial or to lodge a protest against the party's application.
In the circumstances provided for in items (1) and (2) of the preceding paragraph, the People's Court shall inform the party that he or she may apply to the People's Procuratorate for a procuratorial recommendation for retrial or a protest, except for judgments or rulings made upon retrial due to a procuratorial recommendation for retrial or protest lodged by the People's Procuratorate.

Article 386. Where the applicant for retrial proves that the new evidence submitted by him or her falls under any of the following circumstances, the reasons for belated provision of evidence may be found to be established:
(1) The evidence existed before the conclusion of the hearing in the original trial, but was only discovered after the hearing due to objective reasons;
(2) The evidence was discovered before the conclusion of the hearing in the original trial, but could not be obtained due to objective reasons or could not be provided within the prescribed time limit;
(3) The evidence was formed after the conclusion of the hearing in the original trial, and no separate action can be brought based on it.
Where the evidence submitted by the applicant for retrial had already been provided in the original trial, and the People's Court of the original trial did not organize cross-examination and did not use it as the basis for adjudication, the reasons for belated provision of evidence shall be deemed established, unless the People's Court of the original trial did not admit it in accordance with Article 68 of the Civil Procedure Law.

Article 388. Any of the following circumstances resulting in an erroneous outcome of a judgment or ruling shall be determined as the "definite error in the application of law in the original judgment or ruling" provided for in item (6) of Article 207 of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) The applied law is obviously inconsistent with the nature of the case;
(2) The determination of civil liability obviously violates the parties' agreement or legal provisions;
(3) A law that has already ceased to be effective or has not yet come into force is applied;
(4) Provisions on the retroactive effect of laws are violated;
(5) Rules on the application of law are violated;
(6) The legislative intent is obviously contravened.

Article 389. Any of the following circumstances in the course of the hearing in the original trial shall be determined as "depriving the parties of the right to debate" provided for in item (9) of Article 207 of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) The parties are not allowed to express their debate opinions;
(2) The case should have been heard in court but was not heard in court;
(3) The service of the copy of the statement of complaint or the copy of the statement of appeal violates legal provisions, thereby causing the parties to be unable to exercise their right to debate;
(4) Other circumstances in which the parties' right to debate is illegally deprived.

Article 391. The "legal documents" provided for in item (12) of Article 207 of the Civil Procedure Law include:
(1) Judgments, rulings, and mediation statements that have taken legal effect;
(2) Arbitral awards that have taken legal effect;
(3) Notarized debt documents with compulsory execution effect.

Article 400. During the period of examination of an application for retrial, in any of the following circumstances, the examination shall be terminated by a ruling:
(1) The applicant for retrial dies or terminates, and there is no successor to the rights and obligations, or the successor to the rights and obligations declares the waiver of the application for retrial;
(2) In an action for payment, the respondent who has the obligation to pay dies or terminates, and there is no property available for enforcement, and there is no person who shall bear the obligation;
(3) The parties reach a settlement agreement and have fully performed it, unless the parties declare in the settlement agreement that they do not waive the right to apply for retrial;
(4) Another person applies for retrial in the name of a party without authorization;
(5) The original trial court or the court of the next higher level has already ruled to retry the case;
(6) The circumstances provided in Paragraph 1 of Article 381 of this Interpretation exist.

Article 402. When the People's Court holds a hearing for a retrial case, it shall proceed separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) Where the retrial is on the application of a party, the applicant for retrial shall first state the claims and reasons for retrial, then the respondent shall answer, and other parties of the original trial shall express their opinions;
(2) Where the retrial is due to a protest, the protesting organ shall first read the protest document, then the party applying for the protest shall make a statement, then the respondent shall answer, and other parties of the original trial shall express their opinions;
(3) Where the People's Court institutes the retrial ex officio and there is a complainant, the complainant shall first state the claims and reasons for retrial, then the respondent shall answer, and other parties of the original trial shall express their opinions;
(4) Where the People's Court institutes the retrial ex officio and there is no complainant, the original plaintiff or the original appellant shall first make a statement, then other parties of the original trial shall express their opinions.
With respect to the circumstances provided in Items 1 to 3 of the preceding paragraph, the People's Court shall require the parties to clarify their claims for retrial.

Article 404. During the retrial proceedings, in any of the following circumstances, the retrial procedure may be terminated by a ruling:
(1) The applicant for retrial withdraws the claims for retrial during the retrial, and the People's Court permits it;
(2) The applicant for retrial, after being summoned by summons, refuses to appear in court without justifiable reasons, or leaves the court midway without permission of the court, and it is treated as withdrawal of the claims for retrial;
(3) The People's Procuratorate withdraws the protest;
(4) The circumstances provided in Items 1 to 4 of Article 400 of this Interpretation exist.
In a case in which the retrial was ruled because of a protest by the People's Procuratorate, if the party applying for the protest falls under the circumstances in the preceding paragraph, and national interests, public interests, or the lawful rights and interests of others are not harmed, the People's Court shall rule to terminate the retrial procedure.
After the termination of the retrial procedure, the original effective judgment for which the People's Court had ruled suspension of enforcement shall automatically resume enforcement.

Article 414. Where a local People's Procuratorate, upon the application of a party, files a procuratorial recommendation for retrial with the People's Court at the same level against an effective judgment or ruling, the court shall accept the recommendation if it meets the following conditions:
(1) The procuratorial recommendation for retrial, the application of the party in the original trial, and relevant evidence materials have been submitted;
(2) The object of the recommended retrial is a judgment or ruling that may be retried in accordance with the Civil Procedure Law and this Interpretation;
(3) The procuratorial recommendation for retrial specifies that the judgment or ruling has the circumstances provided in Paragraph 2 of Article 215 of the Civil Procedure Law;
(4) The circumstances provided in Items 1 and 2 of Paragraph 1 of Article 216 of the Civil Procedure Law are met;
(5) The procuratorial recommendation for retrial has been decided by the Procuratorial Committee of the said People's Procuratorate after discussion.
If the conditions prescribed in the preceding paragraph are not met, the People's Court may suggest that the People's Procuratorate make corrections or withdraw the recommendation; if no correction or withdrawal is made, the People's Court shall notify the People's Procuratorate in writing that it shall not accept the recommendation.

Article 415. Where the People's Procuratorate, upon the application of a party, files a protest against an effective judgment or ruling, and the protest meets the following conditions, the People's Court shall rule to retry the case within thirty days:
(1) The protest document, the application of the party in the original trial, and relevant evidence materials have been submitted;
(2) The object of the protest is a judgment or ruling that may be retried in accordance with the Civil Procedure Law and this Interpretation;
(3) The protest document specifies that the judgment or ruling has the circumstances provided in Paragraph 1 of Article 215 of the Civil Procedure Law;
(4) The circumstances provided in Items 1 and 2 of Paragraph 1 of Article 216 of the Civil Procedure Law are met.
If the conditions in the preceding paragraph are not met, the People's Court may suggest that the People's Procuratorate make corrections or withdraw the protest; if no correction or withdrawal is made, the People's Court may rule not to accept the protest.

Article 427. Where a creditor applies for a payment order and the following conditions are met, the primary people's court shall accept the application and notify the creditor within five days after receiving the application for the payment order:
(1) The claim is for payment of money or negotiable instruments such as bills of exchange, promissory notes, checks, stocks, bonds, treasury bonds, and transferable certificates of deposit;
(2) The money or negotiable instruments claimed have matured and the amount is definite, and the facts and evidence on which the claim is based are stated in writing;
(3) The creditor has no obligation to perform a counter-obligation;
(4) The debtor is within the territory of China and is not whereabouts-unknown;
(5) The payment order can be served on the debtor;
(6) The people's court that received the application has jurisdiction;
(7) The creditor has not applied to the People's Court for pre-litigation preservation.
If the conditions in the preceding paragraph are not met, the People's Court shall notify the creditor within five days after receiving the application that the application is not accepted.
A primary people's court accepting a case of application for a payment order is not limited by the amount of the claim.

Article 428. After accepting the application, the People's Court shall review it by one judge. Upon review, in any of the following circumstances, the court shall rule to reject the application:
(1) The applicant lacks the qualification of a party;
(2) The documentary evidence for payment of money or negotiable instruments does not stipulate overdue interest or liquidated damages or compensation, but the creditor insists on claiming interest or liquidated damages or compensation;
(3) The money or negotiable instruments claimed are illegal gains;
(4) The money or negotiable instruments claimed have not yet matured or the amount is uncertain.
After accepting an application for a payment order, if the People's Court finds that the acceptance conditions provided in this Interpretation are not met, it shall rule to reject the application within fifteen days from the date of acceptance.

Article 430. In any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall rule to terminate the supervision procedure, and if a payment order has been issued, the payment order shall automatically lose effect:
(1) After the People's Court accepts the application for a payment order, the creditor brings an action again on the same creditor-debtor relationship;
(2) The payment order cannot be served on the debtor within thirty days from the date of issuance by the People's Court;
(3) The creditor withdraws the application before the debtor receives the payment order.

Article 435. Upon formal review, if a written objection raised by the debtor falls under any of the following circumstances, the objection shall be found to be established, and the court shall rule to terminate the supervision procedure, and the payment order shall automatically lose effect:
(1) The circumstances of non-acceptance of an application as provided in this Interpretation;
(2) The circumstances of rejection of an application by ruling as provided in this Interpretation;
(3) The circumstances in which the supervision procedure shall be terminated by ruling as provided in this Interpretation;
(4) The People's Court has reasonable doubt as to whether the conditions for issuing a payment order are met.

A public summons notice for accepting an application issued by a People's Court in accordance with Article 226 of the Civil Procedure Law shall specify the following:
(1) the name of the applicant for public summons;
(2) the type, number, face amount, drawer, endorser, holder, payment term and other particulars of the instrument, as well as the type, number, scope of rights, right holder, obligor, exercise date and other particulars of other right certificates for which public summons may be applied;
(3) the period for asserting rights;
(4) the legal consequences of an interested party's failure to assert rights if the instrument or other right certificates are transferred during the public summons period.

The justifiable reasons specified in Article 230 of the Civil Procedure Law include:
(1) the interested party is unable to know the fact of the announcement due to an unexpected event or force majeure;
(2) the interested party is unable to know the fact of the announcement because of restriction on personal liberty, or although the interested party knows the fact of the announcement, he or she is unable to assert rights personally or entrust another person to assert rights on his or her behalf;
(3) the case does not fall under the statutory circumstances for applying for public summons;
(4) the announcement is not made or is not made in the statutory manner;
(5) other objective causes that prevent the interested party from asserting rights with the People's Court before judgment is rendered.

A notarized debt document specified in Article 245, paragraph 2, of the Civil Procedure Law may be determined to be genuinely erroneous under any of the following circumstances:
(1) the notarized debt document is a debt document to which the effect of compulsory execution shall not be conferred;
(2) the party subject to execution failed to appear in person or to entrust an agent to appear before the notary, or otherwise seriously violated the notarization procedures prescribed by law;
(3) the content of the notarized debt document is inconsistent with the facts or violates mandatory provisions of the law;
(4) the notarized debt document does not specify that the person subject to execution agrees to accept compulsory execution upon failure to perform or to fully perform the obligations.
Where the People's Court determines that execution of the notarized debt document is contrary to the public interest, it shall rule not to execute it.
After the notarized debt document is ruled not to be executed, the parties and the interested parties in the notarized matter may bring an action against the dispute over the debt.

The People's Court may determine a case to be a foreign-related civil case under any of the following circumstances:
(1) One or both of the parties are foreigners, stateless persons, foreign enterprises, or organizations;
(2) The habitual residence of one or both of the parties is outside the territory of the People's Republic of China;
(3) The subject matter is located outside the territory of the People's Republic of China;
(4) The legal facts giving rise to, changing, or extinguishing civil relations occur outside the territory of the People's Republic of China; or
(5) Other circumstances under which a case may be determined as a foreign-related civil case.

Where a foreign-related civil case satisfies all of the following circumstances, the People's Court may rule to dismiss the plaintiff's action and inform the plaintiff to bring the action in a more convenient foreign court:
(1) The defendant requests that the case be under the jurisdiction of a more convenient foreign court, or raises an objection to jurisdiction;
(2) There is no agreement between the parties selecting a court of the People's Republic of China for jurisdiction;
(3) The case is not subject to the exclusive jurisdiction of the courts of the People's Republic of China;
(4) The case does not involve the interests of the state, citizens, legal persons, or other organizations of the People's Republic of China;
(5) The principal facts in dispute do not occur within the territory of the People's Republic of China, and the case is not governed by the law of the People's Republic of China, so that the People's Court would encounter significant difficulties in ascertaining the facts and applying the law in adjudicating the case; and
(6) A foreign court has jurisdiction over the case, and it is more convenient for that court to adjudicate the case.
